title: Protect and Preserve as distinct preservation semantics in pallet-assets

doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    `pallet-assets` collapsed `Preservation` into a single `keep_alive` boolean
    (`preservation != Expendable`), so `Protect` and `Preserve` were indistinguishable and
    `Protect`'s documented meaning — the account may not be killed, but its balance may be
    dusted — had no effect.

    `TransferFlags` and `DebitFlags` now carry a `Preservation` in place of `keep_alive`, and
    `Pallet::reducible_balance` and `Pallet::can_decrease` take one too. `Protect` permits the
    sweep exactly where the asset-account entry outlives its balance, which is where
    `dead_account` returns `Keep`: accounts existing by a deposit (`DepositHeld` or
    `DepositFrom`). Accounts existing by a consumer reference or by asset sufficiency still hold
    back `min_balance` under `Protect`, as they do under `Preserve`. `Expendable` and `Preserve`
    are unchanged, so every `TransferFlags`/`DebitFlags` construction site in the pallet — all of
    which pass one of those two — keeps its current behaviour.

    Note that the default `Mutate::transfer` vetoes a dusting withdrawal for any
    `preservation != Expendable` before reaching the pallet, so, as in `pallet-balances`, the
    distinction surfaces through `reducible_balance`, `decrease_balance` and `burn_from` rather
    than through `transfer`.

    The default `fungible::MutateHold::hold` and `fungibles::hold::Mutate::hold` pass `Protect` to
    `decrease_balance` and then recorded the requested `amount` on hold, discarding the figure
    actually debited. That was inert while no implementor could promote a debit under `Protect`;
    with the above it is reachable, and the difference would leave the account without going on
    hold or coming off the supply. Both now record what `decrease_balance` returned, matching
    `transfer_and_hold` in the same files. This is inert for implementors whose `decrease_balance`
    returns exactly `amount`, which includes `pallet-balances`.

    `fungible::Mutate::transfer` and `fungibles::Mutate::transfer` now document that their return
    may exceed `amount` by up to `minimum_balance() - 1`, matching the range already documented on
    `burn_from` and `Unbalanced::decrease_balance`.

- audience: Runtime User
  description: |-
    Placing a hold on an asset account whose remaining balance would fall below the asset's
    minimum now holds the whole balance rather than failing. The extra is held for the same
    reason as the requested amount and is released with it; no funds leave the account.

crates:
- name: frame-support
  bump: minor
- name: pallet-assets
  bump: major
- name: pallet-assets-precompiles
  bump: patch
